I haven't posted to this group for ages (lurked from time to
time) but your post rings some bells. So, here we go.

"I found myself spotting all sorts of stuff in the game that I
thought should be changed. Suddenly nothing was sacred..."

I have played since Hack 1.0.1., ascended all of the current
classes, and unfortunately I really don't have great interests
of playing any more... and the variants - although not bad at
all - don't fix the major flaws (IMHO) of this game:

1. NETHACK CONS
---------------

1.1. The monsters are not playing the same game as the player.

Simply put. I think this is due to the Ancient D&D Rules(TM).
But the longer you play, the more irritating it gets, at least
for me. A sorry example of this is the lich class. When an
enemy, they do all sort of nasty things, but when tamed or
polymorphed into, they are a really sorry sight. Monsters have
unlimited mana, players don't... etc, etc.

Should one wish to correct such a fundamental flaw, I think
a complete rewrite of the game should be done. But still, IMHO
this is the one great flaw of this game, which results in
the fact that the player cannot really have a "party", i.e.
a gang of equal characters, only the player plus "pets".

1.2. The balance of the game is artificially reached.

While overall, the balance of the game is quite OK, I simply
cannot like the way that this state has been reached. And by
this I mean things like "monster difficulty is based on level
and player exp" stuff. I cannot but hate such gamey stuff as
the "protection racket" that relies on the monsters beging
generated low-level since that's how the player is. Make
the enemy harder by level, fine. But take account the players
exp, what is the logic? I see none, have never seen, and
never will.

Of course, this is a direct consequence of the D&D-type
rules, that make the player combat power increase rapidly
with level. Leave the HP, if you must, but there are many
examples and skill systems to counteract this particular
problem.

1.3. Illogical magic system.

Although "magic" can hardly be described as "realistic",
some logic here would be welcome. The first complaint is
of course that monsters do not use the same magic system
as the player. Forgetting spells... failure %... look
around: we have better magic systems almost everywhere.

Slashem does the best it can with this system, but overall,
a complete rewrite would be required to fix this. Make it
Ultima 7 with reagents, whatever.

1.4. Other minor annoyances

Choking on food (come on!! I have really, really hated this
all the way from 1.0.1!!), food poisoning system, resistances
in general, poison in particular, the all-powerful magic
resistance... you name it. Things that we take for granted
but with some deeper thought could be implemented vastly
better. Again, variants try to focus on these, I even like
the "original" Slash (or was it NH+) system better, where it
would require two meals of red dragon meat to get permanent
fire res., although that was far from perfect also.

2. NETHACK PROS
---------------

Well, after the long whine, one must admit that this game has some
very serious pros, after all:

2.1. The graphics and verbality

This is where this game excels. Even with years and years of
game development, the lack of super-neat graphics *and* the
complex verbosity of the game make this piece of software
really unique. Tiles it is, and that is where it should stay.
In this category falls also the most excellent wishing system,
that is not seen anywhere else, or at least I haven't.

2.2. Bones, and lack of save-reload

This is a "one-shot game", and very unique in that, too. But
the bones system is where this really rocks. In what other
game can you meet ghosts of other unfortunate adventurers?
And be slain by the same monsters that slayed them? Also think
about how much "realism" the simple fact that there is no reload,
no second chance, adds to the game? How many of you would
"save scum", even as we all know how it is done? A fantastically
balanced game is what this requires is all that can be
said about it.

2.3. Complexity at the AI & logical level

By this I mean all the ways things can interact with others.
Try levitating over a sink when polymorphed into a floating
eye to see what I mean. Not putting your effort into fancy
graphics, but to the pure logic of the game really, really
pays off.

2.4. Other stuff

Lots of it. Let it be said that the Devteam has done a fantastic
job, no doubt about it.

3. CONCLUSION
-------------

To comment the suggestions of the first poster, I would have
to say: nice, nice, but I have for a long time since come to
the conclusion that to correct the major cons (IMHO) of the
game would need a complete rewrite - from scratch, not less.
Everything else is "just" a tune-up. Maybe a nice one -
hell, I have really enjoyed Slash'EM! - but a tune-up nonetheless.

Note that it would *not* be *hard*, just require a lot of
work and effort, to completely program from scratch and to balance
the game.

Alas, I don't have the time anymore due to personal life matters...

... one can always dream? ;)

